today I want to tell you three things  something that makes me sad something  that makes me mad  and something that gives me hope I spent  some time living in Rwanda with this  family  this is Tati her husband Pasi seek her  oldest daughter Pascaline and their  littlest baby they have five kids all  together and I want to tell you a little  bit about their lives so every day the  morning starts when their oldest son  Camille joins the procession of hundreds  of kids heading to the nearby lake to  bring water home for their family now  the water that Camille brings back is  dirty and it carries the risk of disease  but it's all they have so they cook it  cook with it and they drink it and they  hope for the best and Tati she spends a  huge part of her day in this little  metal Shack you can see behind her  cooking for her family now I would walk  into that Shack and the smoke would be  so thick that it would burn my eyes  within moments but Tati would spend  hours crouched over this open fire just  breathing in the smoke while she cooked  and a lot of the time that adorable baby  was strapped to her back breathing the  smoke right along with her and at 6:30  every day the lights would go out on  their life and anything that Tati hadn't  finished yet whether it was for her  family or her dress business had to be  done by candlelight when I stayed with  them I went to bed every night feeling  completely in awe of this incredible  woman who works harder every single day  than I ever had to work in my entire  life and every night when I would wake  up  and I would hear Tati in the next room  just coughing constantly because of the  decades of smoke that she had been  inhaling to feed her family that made me  want to cry and the thing is there are  products and services out there that can  make tattoos life so much better take  the three needs that I identified in her  life clean water water health  international gets affordable drinking  water to over five million people in  developing countries clean cooking or  Joe cook stove emit no smoke and they're  sold to twenty five thousand today  electricity huffs power gets affordable  electricity to over four hundred  thousand people who live off the grid  and these aren't charities these are  businesses they're selling these  products in communities just like  Tottie's at affordable prices and  they're doing it with profitable  business models but these businesses  aren't coming to Rwanda any time soon  and I don't know if they will in tatoos  lifetime and this makes me really sad  and now I want to tell you what makes me  mad and to do that I'm gonna take you to  Kigali Rwanda is capital he oli is full  of talented passionate people and they  want to be part of Rwanda success story  and I worked in East Africa in  government and impact investing so I've  met a lot of entrepreneurs and they  always sit down and they tell me about  how they want to change the world and I  always ask them three questions  what's your product what's your business  model and how can you prove to me that  it works but these entrepreneurs they've  never been to India they've never seen  Hoss power in action so their answers  were always less compelling and less  developed than existing businesses I  knew about that we're out there solving  best thing  problems in other countries but instead  of bringing these businesses to these  entrepreneurs and helping them figure  out how to adapt them and scale them in  their own communities we were standing  by and we were cheering them on while  they tried to reinvent the wheel and  this made me mad but what gives me hope  is I know we can change this and  changing it starts with breaking down  the problem first what's not a problem  capital the impact investing market is  eight billion dollars and these are just  some of the organizations that invest in  companies like the ones I talked about  and impact investors you can believe  they want to see the social innovation  space scale up just as much as I do so  there are a lot of great conversations  happening right now about what kind of  capital is needed in what stages and in  which sectors to really get the social  enterprise movement to scale up but I  believe that all the capital in the  world isn't gonna fix this problem if we  rely entirely on entrepreneurs to take  their businesses to new countries and  that's because of three reasons first  focus imagine that you're an  entrepreneur in India you've hit the  Holy Grail you've built a business  that's serving a need in your community  and it's doing it profitably what are  you focused on first probably reaching  as many people in your community as you  can and after that probably reaching as  many people in India as you can and that  makes perfect sense it's probably what  you should focus on but it might take 20  or 30 years and meanwhile their  communities like Tottie's whose lives  could be changed by your business but  they're not a second  perspective imagine that you are ready  to scale internationally if you've never  been to Rwanda or Cambodia how would you  know where to start and how would you  find that passionate entrepreneur in  Kigali Orpah Phnom Penh who wants to  solve a same problem and has what it  takes to bring your business to their  country and scale it up third expertise  imagine that you get yourself to Rwanda  you find the perfect market and the  perfect partner you are now entering  completely uncharted territory as a  manager managing a multinational  business is a totally different thing  from managing something small in your  community and you may have no idea how  to take this leap or where to find the  talent that can help you do that so  these three barriers are really big and  a lot of the time you have social  enterprises that reach this proof of  concept phase and they have the  potential to scale geographically and  reach a lot of lives  but they get stuck and when they get  stuck there are a lot of organizations  that are there ready to mentor them and  support them and give them money to get  it over the hump but there aren't enough  capable knowledgeable hands to  physically do the hard work of taking  these businesses to new countries so a  lot of them don't scale for 20 or 30  years and a lot of them never do this  red bar represents millions of  communities just like toddies whose  lives could be changed by existing  businesses but they're not and that red  bar is what I want to do something about  and I want to do something about it by  building a replicator an organization  that focuses entirely on taking  successful business models to new  markets in developing countries a  replicator would first determine which  of these social enterprises has the  greatest potential to scale in new  markets  and each of these dots represents a real  social enterprise that's been successful  in one country but hasn't scaled further  so we would identify replication targets  and then we would physically do the work  of taking them to new markets say East  Africa where Tati lives we would adapt  the business model and we would find  talented managers who can manage those  businesses long term and once we've done  that there would be a blueprint or a  business in a box that would allow those  businesses to keep on growing maybe  across the continent and maybe beyond I  want to make this happen for Tati for  the four billion people like her who  live on less than $4 a day and for the  entrepreneurs who want to change the  world and shouldn't spend their lives  reinventing the wheel if this is  compelling to you I invite you to join  me thank you
 

